</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The series uses daily closes on weekday sessions only, and the September 28 value is the December contract settle. The Federal Reserve raised its target range to 3.75% to 4.00% on September 16.</p>
<p>Diminishing sensitivity, the companion finding in the 1979 paper, means the first few percentage points below the purchase price register more sharply than the next few.</p>
<h2>The Reference Point Is Also a Trap in the Other Direction</h2>
<p>Loss aversion does not only produce selling. The same reference point produces the break-even instinct, the wish to hold until the price returns to the entry level and the loss can be erased on paper. Terrance Odean, in a 1998 study of individual brokerage accounts in the Journal of Finance, found that investors realized 14.8% of their paper gains but only 9.8% of their paper losses, the pattern Hersh Shefrin and Meir Statman had named the disposition effect in 1985. Gold buyers face both errors this week: capitulation at the low, or a refusal to reassess a position whose original rationale no longer describes what drives the price.</p>
<p>Shlomo Benartzi and Richard Thaler added a further mechanism in 1995 with myopic loss aversion: the more often investors evaluate a position, the more losses they perceive and the more risk averse they become. This week supplies two evaluation events, the Wednesday PCE inflation report and the Friday nonfarm payrolls report, which Trading Economics flagged as the next inputs to Federal Reserve policy.</p>
<h2>Narrow Framing Hides How Much of the Canadian Move Is One Event</h2>
<p>The S&amp;P/TSX Composite fell 311.03 points, or 0.87%, to 35,489.86 on Monday, its lowest close since July, with materials, telecoms and financials leading the decline and decliners outnumbering advancers 686 to 294 on the Toronto Stock Exchange, according to Investing.com. In Canadian dollars, gold settled near C$5,844, down 3.76% on the day and below both its 50-day average of C$6,240.69 and its 200-day average of C$6,259.48, according to Gold Stock Canada.</p>
<p>Narrow framing, the tendency Daniel Kahneman and Dan Lovallo documented in 1993 to evaluate each decision in isolation rather than as part of a whole portfolio, makes a 0.87% index decline and a 3.76% gold decline read as two separate events. They are one event with one cause: a rate repricing that lifted the dollar and yields. The Bank of Canada held at 2.25% on September 2 for a seventh straight decision and flagged that upside risks to inflation have increased, and its next decision arrives on October 28, the same day the Federal Reserve is priced to move. The behavioural test for the next 48 hours is whether investors revise the safe-haven label or defend it.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who added gold ETFs or gold miners in August feel let down by an asset they bought for protection, and the discomfort is sharpest because the news was bad on the day gold fell. Clients with modest gold in a balanced portfolio feel unsettled by a 3.5% one-day move in an asset they were told was stable. Clients with no gold exposure are watching the TSX at its lowest close since July and wondering whether the selling will spread.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to walk you through what happened to gold on Monday, because it fell 3.5% on a day when the news was worse, not better. Gold responds to interest rates and the U.S. dollar more directly than it responds to headlines. Oil rose after the talks with Iran stalled, markets now see roughly 70% odds of another U.S. Federal Reserve rate hike on October 28, and Treasury yields climbed to levels last seen in 2007. Those forces make gold more expensive to hold, and it sold off. Anyone who bought in late August is down about 8%, and gold is roughly 26% below its January high. That is a real loss and I am not going to minimize it. What has changed is the reason gold does well in the short run, not the reason it sits in your portfolio. Let us look at the role it plays in your whole plan and decide together whether that role still fits. I would like us to make that decision after the Wednesday inflation report and the Friday jobs report rather than on a Monday close. Can we book thirty minutes on Friday afternoon, after the jobs report?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who bought gold ETFs, bullion certificates or TSX gold and silver miners during August, retirees drawing income from portfolios where gold was positioned as ballast, and clients whose materials weighting exceeds the limit in their investment policy statement.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Balanced portfolios with a modest gold allocation and a bond sleeve, since the 10-year U.S. Treasury yield at 5.234% is pressuring bond prices at the same time.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with cash on the sidelines and a long horizon, and clients holding short-term GICs or cash inside a TFSA or RRSP who now earn higher yields while waiting.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull the list of clients holding gold ETFs, bullion certificates or TSX gold and silver miners and record each purchase date.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Compare each holding with the August 28 futures close of US$4,529.90 to identify who is down 8% or more.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check which client files describe gold as a hedge and confirm the written purpose in each investment policy statement.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag accounts where TSX materials exposure exceeds the policy limit.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Book calls for Friday afternoon, after the Wednesday PCE report and the Friday jobs report are both out.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document every conversation and the client stated purpose for the gold position in the file.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What happened to gold this week and what it means for your plan<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for speaking with me today. As promised, here is a short summary.<br><br>
Gold fell 3.5% on Monday, September 28, even though the news out of the Middle East worsened. Oil rose after talks between the United States and Iran stalled, and markets now see roughly 70% odds of another U.S. Federal Reserve rate hike on October 28. Higher expected interest rates make gold more expensive to hold, which is why it fell.<br><br>
Two data releases arrive this week, the U.S. PCE inflation report on Wednesday and the U.S. jobs report on Friday. I suggest we review the role gold plays in your plan after both are out, and I have set aside time on [Day and Time] for that conversation. You do not need to decide anything before we speak.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors who bought gold ETFs in August:</strong> they entered near the top of a US$17.1 billion monthly inflow, own the loss alone and have no one to ask whether the hedge is broken.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners holding gold or gold miners in a corporate investment account:</strong> a decline of this size changes the corporate tax and liquidity picture they had assumed.</p>
<p><strong>Self-directed retirees with gold or TSX miners inside an RRSP or RRIF:</strong> a falling balance now interacts with mandatory withdrawals.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Gold fell 3.5% on Monday while oil rose and the war headlines got worse, which is the opposite of what a war hedge is supposed to do. I am calling people who own gold to walk through why that happened and what it says about the role gold should play.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Investors who bought gold this summer bought a story: protection against war. The price on Monday showed that gold is set by interest rates and the dollar, and that the story and the price can separate on the very day the story seems most relevant. Someone managing their own money has no one to test the label against and faces both errors at once, selling at the low or refusing to reassess.</p>
<p>The advisor offers a structure for the decision: what job the position was hired to do, what drives it, and how it fits the whole portfolio. That structure matters most in the days before the Wednesday PCE report and the Friday jobs report, when evaluation is frequent and emotion runs highest.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>1. When did you buy your gold, and what were you hoping it would do for you?</p>
<p>2. Did Monday move look consistent with that purpose, or did it surprise you?</p>
<p>3. How much of your total portfolio is in gold or gold miners today?</p>
<p>4. Where is it held, in an RRSP, a TFSA, a corporate account or a non-registered account?</p>
<p>5. Who do you talk to when a position moves against you like this?</p>
<p>6. What would you need to see to decide to add to it, hold it or reduce it, and have you written that down?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A question about the gold in your self-directed account<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
Gold fell 3.5% on Monday while oil rose and the Middle East headlines worsened, the opposite of what many investors expect from a war hedge. That raises a fair question for anyone who bought gold this summer: what job was it hired to do, and is it doing that job?<br><br>
I would be glad to walk through a short framework for answering that, in fifteen minutes and with no obligation. Would [Day] or [Day] work?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
