VALUES (
  '2026/09/29/boc-market-prices-hikes-core-inflation-two-percent',
  'economy', 'article',
  'Markets Price About 100 Basis Points of Bank of Canada Hikes Against Core Inflation of 2.0%',
  'Headline inflation sits at the top of the 1% to 3% range because gasoline is up 22.8%, and the gap between that energy signal and the rate path is where the October 28 decision lands.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Headline CPI held at 3.0% in August,</strong><span> the top of the Bank of Canada 1% to 3% control range.</span></li>
<li><strong>Core measures averaged 2.0%,</strong><span> so the gap is energy: gasoline is up 22.8% from a year ago.</span></li>
<li><strong>Money markets price roughly 100 basis points of Canadian hikes over 12 months,</strong><span> while most bank economists expect a freeze through 2026.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Government of Canada 10-year yield is 3.94%,</strong><span> pulled higher by a U.S. 10-year yield at 5.22%.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Bank decides on October 28,</strong><span> the same day as the Federal Reserve and with a new forecast.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>Canadian consumer prices rose 3.0% in August from a year earlier, unchanged from July and at the ceiling of the Bank of Canada 1% to 3% control range, Statistics Canada reported on September 14. The measures the Bank prefers told a different story: the average of CPI-trim and CPI-median was 2.0%, unchanged from July, according to TD Economics. The distance between those two numbers is one full percentage point, and almost all of it is energy.</p>
<p>Money markets are pricing the headline, not the core. The Bank held its policy rate at 2.25% on September 2 for a seventh consecutive decision, and markets carry a partial hike for December and roughly 100 basis points of increases over the next 12 months, according to nesto.ca. Most bank economists expect the rate to stay frozen through 2026 and to rise first in 2027.</p>
<h2>What the Core Measures Are Actually Saying</h2>
<p>Gasoline prices were 22.8% higher than a year earlier in August, down from 25.7% in July, according to TD Economics. That single component lifts the headline while the rest of the basket behaves. Shelter inflation was 1.5%, up from 1.3%, with rents rising 2.8% after 2.5% in July, and grocery prices rose 2.8% after 3.1%, the first reading below headline inflation in nearly two years.</p>
<p>Headline inflation has moved from 1.7% in July 2025 to 3.0% in August 2026, with a peak of 3.2% in May, and the series crossed the 3% line only after the Middle East war began in late February. The figures below show that path against the 2% target and the last Bank rate cut.</p>
<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
<div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
<span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;letter-spacing:0.02em;">CANADA CPI: YEAR OVER YEAR</span>
<span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">3.0%</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#2e7d32;">▲ 1.3 POINTS SINCE JUL 2025</span>
<span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">MONTHLY &nbsp;|&nbsp; JUL 2025 TO AUG 2026</span>
</div>
<div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;">
<script>
(function(){
var _cs = document.currentScript;
var svg = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","svg");
svg.setAttribute("viewBox","0 0 680 300");
svg.setAttribute("width","100%");
var FF = "-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif";
function el(tag, attrs, txt){var e=document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg",tag);for(var k in attrs){e.setAttribute(k,attrs[k]);}if(txt!==undefined){e.textContent=txt;}return e;}
function mk(t, attrs){attrs["font-family"]=FF;return el("text",attrs,t);}
var data=[1.7,1.9,2.4,2.2,2.2,2.4,2.3,1.8,2.4,2.8,3.2,2.8,3.0,3.0];
var xlab={0:"Jul 25",3:"Oct 25",6:"Jan 26",9:"Apr 26",13:"Aug 26"};
var events=[{i:3,label:"LAST BOC CUT OCT 29"},{i:7,label:"WAR BEGINS LATE FEB"}];
var n=data.length;
var margin={left:62,right:24,top:18,bottom:46};
var PW=680-margin.left-margin.right;
var PH=300-margin.top-margin.bottom;
var MT=margin.top;
var vmin=Math.min.apply(null,data),vmax=Math.max.apply(null,data);
var padv=(vmax-vmin)*0.1;
var lo=vmin-padv,hi=vmax+padv;
function xp(i){return margin.left+(i/(n-1))*PW;}
function yp(v){return MT+PH*(hi-v)/(hi-lo);}
function computePillWidth(t,fs){return Math.ceil(t.length*fs*0.58)+10;}
function labelWidth(t,fs){return Math.ceil(t.length*fs*0.68);}
var ticks=[1.5,2.0,2.5];
ticks.forEach(function(t){var y=yp(t);svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left,x2:margin.left+PW,y1:y,y2:y,stroke:"#ececec","stroke-width":"0.5"}));});
var currentVal=data[n-1];
var refs=[{v:3.0,c:"#2e7d32",t:"TOP OF BOC RANGE 3.0%"},{v:2.0,c:"#7a3030",t:"2% TARGET"}];
refs.forEach(function(r){var y=yp(r.v);svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left,x2:margin.left+PW,y1:y,y2:y,stroke:r.c,"stroke-width":"1","stroke-dasharray":"3,3"}));});
var d="";
data.forEach(function(v,i){d+=(i===0?"M":"L")+xp(i).toFixed(1)+" "+yp(v).toFixed(1)+" ";});
svg.appendChild(el("path",{d:d,fill:"none",stroke:"#4a5568","stroke-width":"1.8","stroke-linejoin":"round"}));
svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left,x2:margin.left+PW,y1:MT+PH,y2:MT+PH,stroke:"#d8d8d8","stroke-width":"1"}));
svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left,x2:margin.left,y1:MT,y2:MT+PH,stroke:"#d8d8d8","stroke-width":"1"}));
events.forEach(function(ev){var ex=xp(ev.i);svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:ex,x2:ex,y1:MT,y2:MT+PH,stroke:"#1a3560","stroke-opacity":"0.5","stroke-width":"1","stroke-dasharray":"2,3"}));});
var lastX=xp(n-1);
var lastY=yp(currentVal);
svg.appendChild(el("circle",{cx:lastX,cy:lastY,r:4,fill:"#4a5568"}));
var pillText="3.0%";
var pillW=computePillWidth(pillText,9);
var pillH=16;
var pillX=lastX-pillW-6;
var pillY=lastY-pillH/2;
if(pillX<margin.left){pillX=margin.left;}
svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:pillX,y:pillY,width:pillW,height:pillH,rx:2,fill:"#e8a825"}));
svg.appendChild(mk(pillText,{x:pillX+pillW/2,y:pillY+pillH/2+3.5,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"9","font-weight":"700",fill:"#111111"}));
ticks.forEach(function(t){svg.appendChild(mk(t.toFixed(1)+"%",{x:margin.left-6,y:yp(t)+3,"text-anchor":"end","font-size":"8.5",fill:"#aaaaaa"}));});
refs.forEach(function(r){if(Math.abs(r.v-currentVal)/currentVal>=0.03){svg.appendChild(mk(r.t,{x:margin.left+10,y:yp(r.v)-10,"text-anchor":"start","font-size":"7","font-weight":"700",fill:r.c}));}});
events.forEach(function(ev){
var ex=xp(ev.i);
var evW=labelWidth(ev.label,7);
var crowded=events.some(function(o){return o.i!==ev.i&&Math.abs(xp(o.i)-ex)<85;});
var nearRight=(ex+evW+3)>(margin.left+PW);
var anchor=(crowded||nearRight)?"end":"start";
var offset=(crowded||nearRight)?-3:3;
var yStart=crowded?MT+50:MT+20;
svg.appendChild(mk(ev.label,{x:ex+offset,y:yStart,"text-anchor":anchor,"font-size":"7","font-weight":"700",fill:"#1a3560"}));
});
for(var k in xlab){svg.appendChild(mk(xlab[k],{x:xp(parseInt(k,10)),y:MT+PH+14,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"8",fill:"#999999"}));}
_cs.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Statistics Canada, Consumer Price Index, August 2026 release of September 14; monthly series via WOWA.ca; Bank of Canada. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Values are rounded to one decimal. The top of the range is the upper limit of the Bank of Canada 1% to 3% inflation control range, and the last policy rate cut took the rate to 2.25%.</p>
<h2>Why the Bond Market Is Pricing a Different Bank of Canada</h2>
<p>The Federal Reserve raised its target range to 3.75% to 4.00% on September 16, its first increase since 2023, and the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield reached 5.234% on Monday, according to CNBC. The Government of Canada 10-year yield was 3.94% on Tuesday against 5.22% for the U.S. benchmark, according to Trading Economics, a gap of about 129 basis points. Canadian yields tend to follow U.S. yields when the U.S. moves first, which is why they can rise without Canadian data justifying it.</p>
<p>The transmission runs to mortgages. The 5-year Government of Canada yield rose about a quarter of a percentage point in the week to September 11, and lenders raised fixed mortgage rates by 20 basis points to nearly 100 basis points, according to Canadian Mortgage Trends. Borrowers renewing five-year fixed terms face those quotes, while the policy rate that drives variable-rate products and prime, at 4.45%, has not moved.</p>
<h2>The October 28 Decision Carries Both Risks</h2>
<p>Second-quarter GDP grew at a 3.3% annualized rate, according to Statistics Canada, which reduces the case for further easing. The Bank July forecast still projected growth of 0.7% for 2026, according to nesto.ca, so the economy and the forecast are not telling the same story either. Marc Ercolao of TD Economics expects the Bank to hold through the rest of the year, and Leslie Preston of TD wrote that current data does not support aggressive hikes.</p>
<p>The Bank publishes its next Monetary Policy Report on October 28, the same day the Federal Reserve is priced to decide, and releases its Business Outlook Survey on October 19. The Bank has said it is prepared to adjust policy as needed and that upside risks to inflation have increased. The question the October report answers is whether the energy shock has begun to reach the core measures, which are the only data that would move the Bank ahead of the bond market.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients renewing a mortgage in the next year feel exposed, because bond yields are pushing fixed quotes up while the policy rate has not moved. Clients holding GICs and bond funds feel confused: yields are rising, but bond fund values are falling. Retirees drawing income feel uncertain about whether higher rates are good news or a warning.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Here is what is happening with interest rates, and what it means for you. Inflation in Canada is 3.0%, but that number is inflated by gasoline, which is up nearly 23% from a year ago. The Bank of Canada looks at core measures that strip out the volatile items, and those are at 2.0%, right on target. The bond market is pricing in rate hikes anyway, mostly because U.S. yields are rising after the Federal Reserve raised rates on September 16. Most bank economists expect the Bank of Canada to hold at 2.25% through the end of the year. The Bank makes its next decision on October 28. Until then, the practical effects are on longer-term rates: fixed mortgage quotes have risen and bond prices have fallen. I would like to review how your plan responds to a few rate scenarios, including your renewal date and your cash flow needs, so that October 28 is not a surprise.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with a fixed mortgage renewing in the next 12 months, holders of long-term bond funds, and retirees who hold longer-duration bonds for income.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Variable-rate borrowers, whose payments depend on the policy rate and are unchanged today but exposed if the Bank raises rates in 2027, and balanced-portfolio clients whose bond and equity sleeves are both under pressure.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Savers with cash in GICs or high-interest accounts, who can lock in higher yields, and clients with money to invest at higher starting bond yields.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">List clients with fixed mortgage renewals in the next 12 months and note each renewal date.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients holding long-duration bond funds and record the fund duration.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm the cash reserve for each retiree in drawdown covers at least the next 12 months of withdrawals.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review GIC maturities in the next 90 days and prepare reinvestment options.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Mark October 19, the Business Outlook Survey, and October 28, the Monetary Policy Report, in the calendar.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document each rate-scenario conversation in the client file.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What the rate signals mean for your plan<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for speaking with me today. Here is a short summary.<br><br>
Canadian inflation was 3.0% in August, mostly because of gasoline prices. The measures the Bank of Canada prefers were 2.0%. The bond market is pricing rate increases, but most bank economists expect the Bank to hold its policy rate at 2.25% through the end of the year. The next decision is on October 28.<br><br>
Longer-term rates have already moved higher, which affects fixed mortgage quotes and bond prices. I would like to review your renewal date, your cash needs and your bond holdings against a few rate scenarios. I have set aside time on [Day and Time] for that review.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors holding bond funds or long-term bonds:</strong> falling bond prices while yields rise confuses self-directed investors, and no one is available to explain duration.</p>
<p><strong>Homeowners with fixed mortgages renewing within 12 months:</strong> quotes have moved 20 to nearly 100 basis points, and they are deciding between fixed and variable without a rate view.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with floating-rate credit lines:</strong> a rate path that may turn upward in 2027 changes their borrowing and cash reserve decisions.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Canadian inflation is 3.0%, but core inflation is 2.0%, and the bond market is pricing rate hikes the Bank of Canada may not deliver. I am calling homeowners and investors who are renewing or reinvesting in the next year to walk through what that gap means.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Headlines about inflation and rates point in different directions this month. Headline inflation is at the top of the Bank range, core inflation is on target, markets price hikes and economists expect a hold. An investor without a framework sees only the loudest number and tends to react to it.</p>
<p>The advisor connects the pieces: which number the Bank actually follows, how U.S. yields reach Canadian mortgages and bond funds, and what the plan looks like under a few rate paths. That is worth having before October 28, not after.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>1. When does your mortgage renew, and what rate type are you considering?</p>
<p>2. How much of your portfolio is in bonds or bond funds, and do you know their duration?</p>
<p>3. Do you have GICs maturing in the next 90 days?</p>
<p>4. How would a higher payment at renewal affect your monthly cash flow?</p>
<p>5. Who helps you decide between locking in and staying flexible?</p>
<p>6. What would you need to see from the Bank of Canada to change your plan?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Inflation is 3.0% but core inflation is 2.0%: what it means for your renewal<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
Canadian inflation held at 3.0% in August, but the core measures the Bank of Canada follows were 2.0%, and bond markets are pricing rate hikes anyway. That gap is already moving fixed mortgage quotes and bond fund prices.<br><br>
I would be glad to walk through what it means for your renewal or your bond holdings, in fifteen minutes and with no obligation. Would [Day] or [Day] work?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
