<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Daily closes. The Labour Day session of September 7 is excluded. The index closed at 35,490 on September 28, 2.3% below its September 22 close of 36,336.</p>
<h2>The Calendar That Decides the Next Move</h2>
<p>Yahoo Finance flagged a data-heavy week: the PCE inflation report on Wednesday and September employment figures on Friday. Markets are pricing roughly 70% odds of a second Federal Reserve hike at the October 28 meeting, according to CME FedWatch data cited by Trading Economics. A hotter PCE reading or a strong payrolls number would push that probability higher and keep pressure on the long end of the curve.</p>
<p>The Canadian consequence runs through two channels. A higher U.S. 10-year yield raises the hurdle for every dividend and income stock on the TSX, and gold and base metals have been the first casualties of the rate response. Energy is the offset, and it holds only while oil stays near US$93 or higher. Diplomatic progress in the Iran talks would lower oil and inflation, but it would also remove the one sector that rose on Monday.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients see the TSX at its lowest close since July and read it as the start of something larger. Retirees who hold dividend stocks feel a second worry, because a 5.2% Treasury yield makes safe income look competitive with their equity income. Clients with gold or materials exposure feel the loss most directly, and those who bought gold as protection feel it was supposed to behave differently.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Canadian market fell just under 1 percent on Monday and closed at its lowest level since July. That is a modest move in context: the index is about 2 percent below its recent high and still up more than 18 percent from a year ago. The cause was not a Canadian problem. U.S. government bond yields rose above 5 percent, the highest in almost twenty years, and that lowers what investors will pay for stocks everywhere. Gold and base metals sold off on the same pressure, which is why materials fell. The next events that matter are U.S. inflation data on Wednesday, the jobs report on Friday and the Federal Reserve decision on October 28. Your plan was built for periods like this. I would like to review your holdings that are most sensitive to interest rates and confirm the plan is still right for you.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients concentrated in gold and materials, retirees relying on dividend stocks and preferred shares, and clients holding long-duration bonds bought before yields rose.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Balanced-portfolio clients, who lost on equities but earn a higher yield on new bond and GIC purchases.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients holding cash who are ready to buy bonds at the highest yields in years, and clients with Canadian energy exposure.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients whose portfolios hold more than 15% in materials or gold.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">List income-focused clients who hold dividend stocks and preferred shares and review the yield gap to bonds.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review the duration of bond holdings for clients who bought before yields rose.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with idle cash who could add bonds or GICs at current yields.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Mark Wednesday for PCE inflation, Friday for payrolls and October 28 for the Fed decision.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document every conversation about market losses in the client file.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Monday market move and what it means for your plan<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for speaking with me today. Here is a short summary.<br><br>
The Canadian market fell 0.87% on Monday and closed at its lowest level since July. The main cause was a rise in U.S. government bond yields above 5%, the highest since 2007, which lowers what investors will pay for stocks. Gold and base metals fell on the same pressure. The index remains more than 18% higher than a year ago.<br><br>
I would like to review the parts of your portfolio most sensitive to interest rates and confirm the plan still fits your goals. I have set aside time on [Day and Time] for that review.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Income investors with all-equity dividend portfolios:</strong> a 5.2% Treasury yield changes the comparison they made when they built the portfolio.</p>
<p><strong>Investors holding cash on the sidelines:</strong> yields at 20-year highs are the reason many of them waited, and the decision is now in front of them.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors holding gold and mining stocks:</strong> Monday showed that these positions can fall with the market and not against it.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The Canadian market closed at its lowest level since July on Monday, and the cause was a bond market, not a Canadian company. U.S. government yields are above 5 percent for the first time in almost twenty years, and I am calling people to walk through what that means for savings and investments.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>When yields reach a 20-year high, both risk and opportunity change at once. Stocks reprice lower, and new bond and GIC purchases earn more than at any time since 2007.</p>
<p>The advisor compares the return on safer assets with the income from equities, measures the interest rate risk in the existing bond holdings, and sets a plan for the Federal Reserve decision on October 28. That is the analysis an investor cannot get from a headline about the index.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>1. How much of your portfolio is in dividend stocks, and how much of your income depends on them?</p>
<p>2. Have you compared that dividend income with current bond and GIC yields?</p>
<p>3. Do you hold gold or mining stocks, and what role were they meant to play?</p>
<p>4. How much cash are you holding, and what would make you invest it?</p>
<p>5. What would you do if the market fell another 5% after the October 28 decision?</p>
<p>6. Who reviews your interest rate exposure when yields move?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
